1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:20,852
They tried to give me a medal before breakfast. Nigga, I had blood on
my cuff, no sleep in my eyes, and a wife leaning against my shoulder
who had helped liberate a prison before most of the faculty remembered
to put their slippers on. The medal could have waited until somebody
brought eggs. The faculty disagreed.

2
00:00:21,102 --> 00:00:46,219
They assembled in the main hall with a blue ribbon, a gold star, and
enough respectful silence to make Tank suspicious. What these niggas
want? he whispered. Apparently me. Everybody want you. I'm asking what
format. Storm stepped forward. She looked exhausted. Faint silver
lines trembled around her wrists where she had held the casino upright
through the Heart's unstable link.

3
00:00:46,469 --> 00:01:10,579
She had changed into a plain white shirt and dark trousers, but
somehow still looked like the person a storm asked before making
landfall. For exceptional courage in the rescue of the lower-annex
prisoners, she said, and for returning the Heart of the Sky to this
academy— I lifted a hand. She stopped. My people, I said. I pointed at
Shawna. At Tank and Rosa.

4
00:01:10,829 --> 00:01:34,715
At Mercedes, Porsche, Petra, Victor, Pike, Bell, Vega, Marcellus. At
Lena sitting in a chair nearby with a blanket around her shoulders.
Whatever that sentence is about to give me, make enough. Storm looked
down at the medal. Then she smiled. We will. Tank leaned toward Rosa.
Nigga got us jewelry through collective bargaining. You don't know
what that means.

5
00:01:34,965 --> 00:01:57,534
I know I'm collectively about to shine. The room laughed. The freed
prisoners laughed too, some cautiously, some like they were surprised
their bodies still remembered where laughter lived. I let Storm put
the ribbon around my neck. Then I put the medal around Shawna's. She
looked at me. You earned this. Baby, I got you. That was corny as
fuck.

6
00:01:57,784 --> 00:02:22,593
It was also true enough to make her eyes go bright. Breakfast finally
arrived. We ate in the main hall because nobody wanted to leave the
people still being examined by the medical team. Tables filled with
food, borrowed clothes, shoes in every size, and phones people could
use to call somebody who had not known where they were. Tank
personally distributed the shoes.

7
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:46,219
He knelt in front of the gray-suited man, who introduced himself as
Mr. Harlan Moss, fifty-two, a landscape architect whose gift let him
persuade roots to grow through stone. He had been taken after refusing
to work on one of Voss's private properties. Tank offered him two
pairs. Moss looked at them. Both? Nigga, you got two kinds of
occasions.

8
00:02:46,469 --> 00:03:09,818
Moss laughed and chose the polished brown pair for now. He set the
boots beside his chair with the careful pleasure of a man arranging
evidence that tomorrow might exist. His phone call came through while
Tank was lacing the boots for him. A woman filled the screen. Gray
curls, a blue bathrobe, eyes so wide she looked almost angry with him.
Harlan?

9
00:03:10,068 --> 00:03:26,305
He set the phone against a water glass. Missed. Caught it before it
fell. Anne. She covered her mouth. He covered his. For a few seconds
the niggas couldn't say anything at all. Tank stopped touching the
boots. I put my coffee down.

10
00:03:26,555 --> 00:03:50,029
Around us breakfast kept happening, but slowly, as if everybody near
that table had understood there was a voice trying to find its way
home. Anne dropped her hand first. Where the fuck have you been?
Harlan started laughing. Then crying. Then trying to answer both
questions with the same breath. I've had a bad few months. I can see
that. Your hair looks terrible.

11
00:03:50,279 --> 00:04:08,719
He touched it. She began crying too. Nigga, there are people who need
a whole orchestra to tell somebody they love them. That woman had done
it with a complaint about his hair, and Harlan heard every note. He
turned the phone to show her the room. Blankets. Breakfast. Shoes.

12
00:04:08,969 --> 00:04:23,916
My badge, which she studied with the ferocity of a woman deciding
whether to trust the next thing a man in a uniform told her. Did you
find him? she asked me. My wife did. Then put her on. I waved Shawna
over.

13
00:04:24,166 --> 00:04:45,589
She took the phone, sat beside Harlan, and listened to Anne thank her
so many times she finally had to say, Girl, breathe. We got him.
Harlan looked at his boots. She bought me shoes before our wedding, he
told Tank. I'd spent everything on the ring. Tank nodded gravely.
Respectable allocation. They were brown. Like these.

14
00:04:45,839 --> 00:05:09,684
I wanted black because I thought black looked distinguished. What
happened? Harlan looked at Anne on the screen. What do you think
happened? Tank looked at Rosa across the room. Brown shoes. Brown
shoes, Harlan agreed. He told us about their wedding while Anne
corrected him from the phone. It had rained. The roof had leaked over
the little rented chapel's piano.

15
00:05:09,934 --> 00:05:30,926
His brother had put a cooking pot under the leak and called it
percussion. Anne's mother had objected to the music. Anne had walked
down the aisle anyway, carrying her own umbrella, and closed it over
her mother's face just long enough to finish saying yes. That isn't
how it happened, Anne said. Harlan smiled at the screen.

16
00:05:31,176 --> 00:05:41,303
You told your sister you wished it had. My sister talks too much. She
sent me the letter. Anne stopped. Then she laughed so hard the picture
shook.

17
00:05:41,552 --> 00:06:05,671
That was their whole marriage coming through a little glowing
rectangle: the rain, the ridiculous shoes, a letter somebody had kept,
an argument that had survived thirty years because neither of them
wanted to win it enough to stop having it together. I looked at
Shawna. She looked back. Thirty years sounded like something a nigga
ought to earn one morning at a time.

18
00:06:05,921 --> 00:06:29,178
Anne's face steadied on the screen. I'm coming, she said. Harlan
looked around the floating school. It's a little complicated to get
here. Nigga, I spent five months trying to get you back. Don't tell me
about parking. Tank bowed his head so she wouldn't see him laughing.
Pike arranged a ground escort to the academy's landing station. Storm
offered a shuttle.

19
00:06:29,428 --> 00:06:51,083
By the time Anne ended the call, Harlan had said her name so often it
had become the easiest word in the room. He put the phone down gently.
Then he looked at me. I don't know what to give you. Take her dancing.
He looked at the brown shoes. I pointed. Nigga, you already equipped.
He laughed again. This time it came easily.

20
00:06:51,333 --> 00:07:11,842
Tank finished the last knot, stood, and inspected his work. Wedding
shoes, he said. Thirty years late, Harlan answered. Thirty years still
going. Harlan pressed his lips together and nodded. Then he picked up
his fork and ate like a man with somewhere to be. Shawna sat with
Lena. Rosa poured coffee.

21
00:07:12,092 --> 00:07:33,471
The three women spoke low enough that I heard only pieces: songs, five
months, a sister in Denver, a room with a window. None of it needed me
in the middle. I ate my eggs. A nigga can save a room and still let
other people have conversations inside it. Afterward Reiss took us
back beneath the school.

22
00:07:33,721 --> 00:07:58,244
The transfer chamber looked smaller with the lights fully on. Without
Voss's guards and the snow blowing through it, the place showed its
seams: cables enclosed in heavy channels, rails bolted to the floor, a
control platform crowded with dials Reiss touched as if apologizing to
them. The Heart stood in a secured housing behind reinforced glass.

23
00:07:58,494 --> 00:08:21,371
Bell had connected it to the academy's restored support system. It
remained the school's core; we were not putting it in my trunk and
hoping the clouds would understand. Storm stood at the controls. Reiss
showed her the three rings governing the door's alignment. She
listened, asked two questions, and repeated the sequence back without
touching anything.

24
00:08:21,621 --> 00:08:45,484
Only you can hold the boundary stable without drawing too much from
the school, he said. Once I set the coordinates, you open it. I can't
guarantee a second attempt if we close it fully. Then we make one
passage out and one passage back, Storm said. He nodded. A single
operation. After that, the frame comes apart. I looked at the empty
circle.

25
00:08:45,734 --> 00:09:09,340
And we don't come back with everybody suddenly unborn or some shit?
Reiss shook his head. This isn't a road to your own childhood. Voss
connected us to a branch and changed it. The history we came from
still happened. We can't erase the Holocaust by driving through this
machine. The room stayed quiet. We can help people trapped on its
other side, he said.

26
00:09:09,590 --> 00:09:33,916
People who exist there now. That is the choice in front of us. Shawna
looked at me. That's enough. Yeah, I said. It is. Tank ran his hand
along the closed carriage's empty rail. How many? Reiss spread the
depot papers across a workbench. Keller's site held eighty-three adult
prisoners at the last count. Thirty-one were people Voss had sent from
the present.

27
00:09:34,166 --> 00:09:58,392
The rest belonged to that altered year: Jewish prisoners, resistance
members, other adults seized by the Nazi occupation. Some were forced
to maintain the depot. Some were used in the power experiments. Some
were awaiting transport to places whose names did not need to be
written down for the fear to make sense. We don't know if the count
has changed, Reiss said.

28
00:09:58,642 --> 00:10:19,075
Then we count when we get there, Shawna answered. He showed us the
station layout. Not a plan for conquering history. One depot, one
connecting yard, one machine chamber Keller had built around the other
end of Voss's door. A holding building stood beyond the tracks. A
repair shed faced the station.

29
00:10:19,325 --> 00:10:41,169
A road led into the town of Saint-Orage, whose name Reiss said had not
appeared in the records of the world he had grown up in. I studied the
repair shed. People in there work on engines? Some do. Good. People
who know a machine can usually tell you which nigga keeps breaking it.
Pike wanted to send a full patrol squad.

30
00:10:41,419 --> 00:11:01,627
Storm shook her head. Voss was still free somewhere in the surrounding
sky, and the academy had thousands of people to protect. We could take
a small team through. The rest had to hold the way home. I chose Tank,
Shawna, Victor, and Petra. Rosa stood up immediately. No. Tank looked
at her.

31
00:11:01,876 --> 00:11:25,342
Baby— You not disappearing into another century while I wait upstairs
wondering if some dead motherfucker got lucky. He opened his mouth.
She put a finger against his chest. I know what I'm saying. Mercedes
stood beside Petra. Same. Porsche looked around the room. Nigga, y'all
not leaving me to explain all this to the luggage. I looked at Storm.

32
00:11:25,592 --> 00:11:49,428
She looked at the ring, then the vehicles waiting beyond the chamber
doors. The limit is power draw and transport space, she said. Not
whether someone seems impressive on a roster. Good, Shawna said.
Because the roster didn't open the cells. We settled it the way my
people settled things: by deciding what each of us could do and
trusting each other to do it.

33
00:11:49,678 --> 00:12:12,278
Rosa would help move prisoners and keep people together. Mercedes had
worked enough crowded rooms to read a frightened group before it
turned into a crush. Porsche spoke French well enough to make
Marcellus raise both eyebrows when she finally admitted it. My first
serious boyfriend was from Marseille, she said. You never mentioned
that, Tank told her.

34
00:12:12,528 --> 00:12:36,748
Nigga, you never asked me a question that wasn't about where I parked.
Vega would come as the doctor. Reiss would remain with Storm to hold
the return point. Marcellus stayed at Sky High with Lena and the freed
prisoners; he said somebody needed to keep track of people while all
the beautiful idiots went charging through a clock. You calling me
beautiful? Tank asked.

35
00:12:36,998 --> 00:12:59,482
Marcellus looked at him over his glasses. I included you in a group.
Don't get greedy. Bell prepared three vehicles. My dark blue police
Lamborghini. Tank's black Mercedes. A broad academy rescue van with
room for supplies and people who could not walk. They would run on
ordinary ground once through the door.

36
00:12:59,732 --> 00:13:21,142
Their sky-lane receivers would have nothing to hold onto in the other
year. No roads made of light, Bell said. Tank looked almost moved.
Nigga, finally some pavement with manners. We were not taking a convoy
large enough to empty the whole depot in one trip. The papers showed
trucks and rail carriages already there.

37
00:13:21,392 --> 00:13:42,410
Getting the prisoners to the door would depend partly on what they
knew and what we could secure when we arrived. I didn't love
uncertainty. I loved pretending it scared me even less. While Bell
worked, my phone rang again. Knox had seen the prison footage. This
time she didn't begin with a joke. You have what you need?

38
00:13:42,660 --> 00:14:08,276
I looked around the chamber at my people. Shawna speaking with Vega.
Tank listening to Rosa. Victor beside Petra, testing one small spark
between his fingers. Porsche arguing with Bell about whether the
rescue van had a proper heater. Mostly. I asked about equipment,
Captain. So did I. She was quiet for a second. Then she said, Bring
them back. That's the plan. And Aiden?

39
00:14:08,526 --> 00:14:33,324
Yeah? If anybody asks which department sent you, say mine. I smiled.
Chief, you sure you want that paperwork? Nigga, I want the picture. At
noon, Voss tried to take his machine back. The first sign was a low
metallic boom above us. Dust sifted from the ceiling. Storm looked up
sharply. The control lights flickered. Pike's voice burst over the
radio.

40
00:14:33,574 --> 00:14:59,090
Three armed skiffs approaching the lower service bridge. Tank closed
the Mercedes door. This nigga don't know when the party over. I went
toward the bridge with him and Victor. Shawna followed, because
apparently I still needed reminding that marriage was a formation. The
skiffs hovered outside the maintenance windows. Black hulls. Men
crouched behind their rails. A loudspeaker crackled.

41
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:24,443
Surrender the core and release Commander Sable. I looked at the
speaker. Nigga, he asking for refunds. Victor's hands lit. Vega's
voice came over his radio immediately. Brief use. He stared at the
radio. How does she always know? Because she a doctor and you a
patient. That relationship follows a nigga. The first skiff fired into
the bridge. Glass shattered over us.

42
00:15:24,693 --> 00:15:43,362
We dropped behind the stone wall. Tank pulled a maintenance worker
down beside him and held him there while the man tried to explain that
he had only come to change a light. Nigga, the whole light changing.
Stay down. I returned fire through the broken window. The skiff
veered.

43
00:15:43,612 --> 00:16:07,038
Victor sent a short bolt into its steering ring, and the craft spun
away toward an empty service platform. The men aboard scrambled to
hold on. The second skiff came lower, aiming for the transfer
chamber's outer doors. Shawna saw it before I did. They're going
underneath. We ran back down the passage. The chamber doors shook
under the first impact.

44
00:16:07,288 --> 00:16:29,094
Bell stood behind the Heart's reinforced housing with two tools in
each hand and the expression of a man prepared to defend his
profession physically. Move the van, I told him. Where? Away from the
part I'm about to use. I got into the Lamborghini. Shawna got in
beside me. The outer doors buckled inward.

45
00:16:29,344 --> 00:16:51,737
Beyond them the second skiff hung level with the floor, its nose
pressed against the damaged frame. Two gunmen prepared to jump across.
I turned on the police lights. They paused. Nigga, even in the sky, a
man with a gun could still have an unresolved feeling about a traffic
stop. I drove straight at them. Not out into the air.

46
00:16:51,987 --> 00:17:16,009
Into the heavy rolling barrier parked beside the entrance. The
Lamborghini shoved it across the floor and into the skiff's nose. The
craft rocked backward. Its gunmen fell against each other. Bell hit
the emergency shutters, and a steel screen came down between us and
the sky. The skiff crashed against the outside. The third fled before
Storm's wind reached it.

47
00:17:16,259 --> 00:17:39,148
When I got out, Bell stared at the Lamborghini's newest dent. I held
up one finger. That was barely a trip. He covered his face with two
hands and pointed at the van with the other two. Please just go save
people in another year. We finished preparing under guard. That
evening, Shawna and I went back to our room for twenty minutes. We
washed.

48
00:17:39,398 --> 00:18:04,738
Changed. Stood at the balcony looking out over a sky that had somehow
become familiar in four days. She wore dark trousers, boots, and a
blue sweater beneath her jacket. The medal lay on the dresser. Her
ring remained on her hand. Dante's watch ticked against her wrist. My
cross rested outside my shirt. She touched its engraved back. Damn,
boy, she said softly. Magnificent.

49
00:18:04,988 --> 00:18:31,030
She smiled. You really believe that. Baby, you engraved supporting
evidence. She pulled me toward her. We kissed slowly, without an
audience, without somebody announcing that the sky had developed
another emergency. I held the back of her head and felt how much I
wanted all the future waiting after this one difficult tomorrow. You
scared? she asked. Yeah. She nodded. No disappointment. No surprise.

50
00:18:31,280 --> 00:18:52,882
Just her hand tightening in mine. Me too. We stood there another
minute. Then she picked up her jacket. Come on. That woman been
waiting. When we returned to the chamber, everybody was ready. Lena
had come down with Marcellus to see us off. She took Rosa's hands and
sang one clear note. Rosa answered it. No secret code.

51
00:18:53,132 --> 00:19:17,720
Just two singers promising to recognize each other when the door
opened again. Storm stood at the controls. The Heart glowed behind its
glass. One branch, she said. One outward passage. One return. I hold
this side. Reiss holds the alignment. You bring everyone you can back
through the same point. I nodded. She turned the first ring. Blue
light crawled around the frame.

52
00:19:17,970 --> 00:19:42,695
The second ring moved. Cold entered the room before the view did. Then
the metal circle filled with a snow-covered rail yard beneath an
evening sky. A low station. A repair shed. The dark shape of a train.
Beyond them, a building with barred windows and a light burning in one
upper room. The place where somebody had seen us leave. I started the
Lamborghini.

53
00:19:42,945 --> 00:20:04,922
Tank started the Mercedes behind me. The rescue van's engine joined
ours. Shawna reached across and touched my arm. Ready? I looked at
her. At my wife, who could have stayed in a room above the clouds and
had instead chosen this impossible road beside me. Nigga, I said
softly, I been ready for you my whole life.

54
00:20:05,172 --> 00:20:15,989
She laughed once and wiped her eye with the heel of her hand. Then I
put the car in gear. The snow beyond the door began turning blue
beneath my police lights.
